## 3. Using the pattern in MVVM (WPF/WinUI)

In MVVM the `ICommand` interface from `System.Windows.Input` (or `Windows.UI.Xaml.Input`) is used to bind UI actions to view‑model logic.  
Because that interface already contains an `Execute(object)` method, you typically wrap a delegate in a reusable `DelegateCommand` class.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Windows.Input;   // WPF; use Windows.UI.Xaml.Input for UWP/WinUI

public class DelegateCommand : ICommand
{
    private readonly Action<object> _execute;
    private readonly Predicate<object> _canExecute;

    public DelegateCommand(Action<object> execute, Predicate<object> canExecute = null)
    {
        _execute   = execute ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(execute));
        _canExecute = canExecute;
    }

    public bool CanExecute(object parameter) => _canExecute?.Invoke(parameter) ?? true;

    public void Execute(object parameter) => _execute(parameter);

    public event EventHandler CanExecuteChanged
    {
        add    => CommandManager.RequerySuggested += value;
        remove => CommandManager.RequerySuggested -= value;
    }
}
```

**View‑Model**

```csharp
public class LightViewModel
{
    public ICommand ToggleCommand { get; }

    private readonly Light _light = new Light();

    public LightViewModel()
    {
        ToggleCommand = new DelegateCommand(_ => ToggleLight());
    }

    private void ToggleLight()
    {
        if (_light.IsOn) _light.TurnOff();
        else            _light.TurnOn();
    }
}
```

**XAML**

```xml
<Button Content="Toggle Light"
        Command="{Binding ToggleCommand}"/>
```

That’s literally the Command pattern in action, but inside a UI framework. The button doesn’t know *how* the command works – the view‑model does – and it can be reused wherever needed.

